WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.688
Muscle contraction begins with electrical excitation.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.230
The supplied sequence reaches
voltage-sensitive receptors in the transverse

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.377
tubules and opens ryanodine channels.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.900
Released calcium enables actin to bind myosin.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.410
Now watch thin filaments move inward relative to thick

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:16.856
filaments.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.250
Their overlap increases and the sarcomere shortens.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.530
The motion represents cross-bridge
activity, not shortening of the

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.418
individual filaments.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.580
The packet identifies a
continuing requirement for ATP and

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:30.580
phosphocreatine.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.310
During relaxation, calcium returns
to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, cross-bridges

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.209
detach, and the sarcomere lengthens.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.013
Follow the reverse motion.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.550
This film uses a textbook figure with schematic motion

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.190
to separate excitation,
coupling, contraction, and relaxation.
